# LF everywhere: this project asserts byte-identical output and hashes metadata
# files for provenance. core.autocrlf=true on Windows would otherwise flip these
# to CRLF on checkout and change both the CSV bytes and the metadata hashes.
*.csv  text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.sql  text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# Build-scaffolding review diffs: `git apply` rejects a patch whose line endings
# no longer match the blobs it was generated against.
*.diff text eol=lf

# The renderers write SVG with newline="\n" and --check compares the committed file
# byte-for-byte. That check currently survives a CRLF checkout only because
# Path.read_text normalises newlines on the way in -- i.e. by accident, not by
# design. nl_dataflow.svg was already sitting in the working tree as CRLF.
*.svg  text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf

# Binary, and stated so rather than left to git's heuristic. A PNG or PDF that gets
# newline-translated is a corrupt file, and the diagrams are committed output.
*.png  binary
*.pdf  binary
